   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "80F2BgVNjb0k"
   },
   "source": [
    "# Module 2: Appreciating, Interpreting and Visualizing Data\n",
    "## Lab 1: Basic Plots\n",
    "---"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "_yzMBMsyCQub"
   },
   "source": [
    "# Introduction: Why Visualize Data?\n",
    "\n",
    "It is often said, **\"Data is only as good as it is presented.\"** Countless studies show how our brain is wired for the visual, processing images and patterns far faster than tables of numbers. Before we write a single line of code for a complex machine learning model, **visualizing the data is a critical first step**. It allows us to:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Appreciate the scale and scope of our data.\n",
    "- Interpret relationships, patterns, and distributions within the data.\n",
    "- Visualize potential problems like outliers or missing values.\n",
    "\n",
    "In this lab, you will learn simple yet powerful ways to **visualize your data using Python's most popular libraries**. Understanding these plots will help you decode the various features in a dataset and understand their impact on what you are trying to predict.\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "2bbUeZ2uD-DP"
   },
   "source": [
    "![image.png](data:image/png;base64,)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "azRjmBq6E2es"
   },
   "source": [
    "## Our Toolkit: Matplotlib and Seaborn\n",
    "\n",
    "Data visualizations in Python can be done via many packages. We’ll start with **Matplotlib**, the foundational plotting library, and then move to **Seaborn**, which is built on top of Matplotlib and allows us to create more statistically-focused and aesthetically pleasing plots with less code.  \n",
    "\n",
    "- **Matplotlib:**  \n",
    "  It is a robust 2D plotting library that provides a high degree of control over every aspect of a figure. It's like having a full box of crayons, where you can draw and customize anything you want.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Seaborn:**  \n",
    "  This is a higher-level library that simplifies the creation of common, informative statistical plots. It comes with beautiful default styles and palettes to make your charts look great right out of the box.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "#### **Anatomy of a Matplotlib Figure**"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "yvuxnR8fGKYv"
   },
   "source": [
    "![image.png](data:image/png;base64,
... 361804 bytes omitted ...
",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 2400x700 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(24, 7))\n",
    "sns.violinplot(data=X, x=\"make\", y=y, hue=\"make\", palette=\"Set3\", dodge=False, legend=False, inner=\"quart\", ax=ax)\n",
    "ax.set(title=\"Car maker vs. price — violin plot\", xlabel=\"Car maker\", ylabel=\"Price\")\n",
    "ax.tick_params(axis=\"x\", labelrotation=45)\n",
    "fig.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "cZhB2e7RAtrJ"
   },
   "source": [
    "### Interpretation of the Violin Plot\n",
    "\n",
    "Each violin shows the distribution of prices for one manufacturer. Wider sections represent price ranges with more cars; the inner quartile marks show the median and middle half of the data. The plot should be read brand by brand: a violin that is higher on the price axis has more expensive cars, while a wider or longer violin indicates greater variation. The plot complements the box plot by showing the shape of each distribution, including concentrations and tails.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "p_V_Qnm3aJ_-"
   },
   "source": [
    "Another plot type are the **Swarm Plots** (Every Point Matters)\n",
    "\n",
    "A Swarm Plot is like a scatter plot, but it adjusts the position of the points to prevent them from overlapping. This allows you to see the distribution while still plotting every single data point. It's great for smaller datasets where you want to see individual observations. Unlike strip plots, swarm plots attempt to avoid obscuring points by calculating non-overlapping positions instead of adding random jitter. This sort of gives them appearance of a swarm of bees, or perhaps a honeycomb."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 9,
   "metadata": {
    "colab": {
     "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/",
     "height": 384
    },
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-10T08:05:43.835171Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-10T08:05:43.835171Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-10T08:05:44.866638Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-10T08:05:44.864042Z"
    },
    "id": "8773w_ZV3P3D",
    "outputId": "8c3fa336-9f2b-4869-b5e7-1a86ee8818f1"
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
